# tools/gaussian_witness.rish -- the Gaussian smooth blur proves itself on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the SECOND convolution rung, standing on image/blur.rye's box mean
# (tools/blur_witness.rish). A single box blur softens a picture bluntly: it spreads a point of light into a flat-top
# plateau and leaves a faint boxy ring on a straight edge. A Gaussian is the smooth blur a designer reaches for -- frosted
# glass, a soft drop shadow, gentle depth-of-field -- where a point spreads into a rounded PEAK that falls off evenly with
# no plateau and no ring.
#
# The crux this witness proves is that a Gaussian needs NO Gaussian kernel and NO float: by the central limit theorem a box
# mean run a few times converges to a Gaussian, so gaussian is just blur.box_blur applied `passes` times. On metal: radius 0
# is the identity at any pass count; a single pass degenerates to box_blur byte-for-byte (the general verb contains the box
# as its 1-pass case); the default equals the three-pass general form; a lone white dot on a 9px row spreads to the exact
# bell [0,9,28,57,66,57,28,9,0], a strictly monotonic peak, where the SAME row under one box blur is the flat-top plateau
# [0,0,0,85,85,85,0,0,0] (center tied to neighbor) -- same input, two signatures, the whole reason this rung exists; the 2D
# peak is symmetric on both axes; a solid image stays solid edge to edge; alpha blurs into a peak like any channel; the
# blurred image round-trips through the open QOI codec byte-for-byte; an over-max radius, a zero or over-max pass count, and
# an empty source refuse by name having allocated nothing; the same blur is deterministic. No network, no key, no funds --
# the smooth softening a surface runs the day it wants frosted glass or a soft shadow, on our own open geometry.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/gaussian_witness.rish

say "gaussian: Language EN -- Season G convolution; an RGBA image smooth-blurred by a stack of box means, the second convolution rung standing on the box floor, the shape a frosted panel and a soft shadow both want."
say "gaussian: Style Radiant -- a Gaussian needs no Gaussian kernel and no float; by the central limit theorem a box mean run a few times converges to a Gaussian, so gaussian composes blur.box_blur passes times, each pass reading the last, three passes the display-quality standard; every property the box proved (separable, integer-only, deterministic, clamped edges, alpha averaged) carries up unchanged."
say "gaussian: Lens TAME -- u32 in-memory counts, the radius bounded by gaussian_max_radius (the box's own ceiling) and the pass count by gaussian_max_passes; each function states its invariants positively; the source, radius, and pass count guarded once up front so a refusal allocates nothing; each intermediate buffer freed as the next pass replaces it so only the caller's result outlives the call; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/gaussian"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/gaussian.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "gaussian: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "gaussian: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "gaussian: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-gaussian" else "gaussian: selftest did not print the GREEN line"

say "gaussian: GREEN -- the smooth blur rounds a point into a bell where the box left a plateau, proven on metal atop the box floor, the shape a frosted panel and a soft shadow stand on."
